occupationalSummary:
&lt;p&gt;This occupation is found in the electrical supply industry. This industry is made up of mainly large employers who are responsible for the transmission and distribution of electricity around the country, through networks of overhead lines, underground cables and substations, delivering electricity from where it is generated to where it is used.&amp;nbsp; Electrical power network control engineers may work on the high voltage or the extra high voltage network. Different parts of the network have their own role, procedures and equipment.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is a core and options apprenticeship.&amp;nbsp; An apprentice must be trained and assessed on the core and one option relevant to their role. The options are: distribution network control engineer, system control engineer or transmission control engineer.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The electrical supply industry operates seven days a week, 365 days a year to make sure that homes and businesses always have the electricity they need, as a result, control engineers usually work shifts, carrying out planned work and responding to faults or incidents such as storms or fires to restore power quickly.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The broad purpose of the occupation is to to manage and control the network to ensure a safe, secure and reliable supply. Electrical power network control engineers work in a network control room and are responsible for monitoring network security and availability on the high voltage and extra high voltage networks. They manage safe access to the network to enable planned and unplanned work to take place. This could include maintenance, replacement work, new connections and fault management for network equipment. They coordinate work between site engineers and the control room. Control engineers must provide an immediate response to losses of supply to ensure that they comply with their licence obligations and that customer interruptions and customer minutes lost targets are met. They also carry out analysis to identify faults where interventions are needed and carry out emergency procedures to manage incidents safely. New innovations mean more connections being made into the network and the control engineer has an important role to make sure that work and power flow is coordinated safely.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;System control engineers oversee the real-time flow of electricity across the transmission network. They continuously balance supply and demand to maintain stability, security, and compliance with technical limits. By utilising advanced tools and live data, they make decisions to ensure uninterrupted power supply and facilitate the transition to a low-carbon energy system.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In their daily work, an employee in this occupation a wide range of stakeholders such as distribution network operators, transmission network operator, contractors, planning departments, regulators and customers to give operational information and therefore must be able to maintain complex relationships through excellent communication and negotiation skills. Electrical power network control engineers typically report to a control room manager or shift supervisor.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
